---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { profile } from "../data/profile";
---

<Base title="About" description={`About ${profile.name}.`}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    {profile.summary.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}

    <p>
      The through-line in most of what I build is governance that does not rely
      on people remembering to be careful. A policy written in a wiki is a
      suggestion; a policy enforced in the query path is a guarantee. Most of my
      recent work — sensitive-data classification, certified-metric enforcement,
      approval-gated migrations, identifier rotation for deletion — is some
      version of moving a rule from documentation into the critical path.
    </p>

    <p>
      I am also interested in where language models genuinely help with this and
      where they do not. They are good at assembling context and drafting
      structure, and they are not accountable for a corrupted production table
      or a misclassified PII column. The systems I have had most success with
      lean hard on the first and keep a human firmly attached to the second.
    </p>

    <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
    <p>
      I am on <a href={profile.links.github}>GitHub</a> and
      <a href={profile.links.linkedin}>LinkedIn</a>, and reachable at
      <a href={`mailto:${profile.links.email}`}>{profile.links.email}</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
